📌 1. Understanding Terraform Providers

A Terraform provider is a plugin that allows Terraform to interact with cloud platforms and external services.
Providers help Terraform communicate with AWS, Azure, GCP, Kubernetes, and other platforms.

Key Highlights:
1️⃣ Providers enable Terraform to manage infrastructure.
2️⃣ Providers contain resource and data source definitions.
3️⃣ Each provider has its own version and configuration.
4️⃣ Terraform downloads the required provider plugins during initialization.

Example – Required Providers:
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 6.0"
    }
  }
}

Example – AWS Provider Configuration:
provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

📂 2. Understanding Terraform Configuration Files
Learned how to organize Terraform configuration using different .tf files.
1️⃣ main.tf --> Defines infrastructure resources such as EC2.
2️⃣ providers.tf --> Contains AWS provider configuration.
3️⃣ terraform.tf --> Contains Terraform settings and required provider declarations.

